.\" eictar(1) - the encrypted individually-compressed tar
.\" View it with: man ./doc/eictar.1
.TH EICTAR 1 "2026-09-24" "eictar" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
eictar \- archive files, each compressed and optionally encrypted on its own
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eictar
.B \-c
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-r | \-u
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-t | \-x | \-\-verify
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ...]
.br
.B eictar
.B \-\-delete
.B \-f
.I archive
.IR pattern ...
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-\-compact | \-\-change\-passphrase | \-\-repair | \-\-info
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-\-list\-codecs | \-\-show\-config | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B eictar
works like
.BR tar (1),
but it compresses each member separately, and it can encrypt each member
separately. An index at the end of the archive lists every member, so a
listing reads only the end of the file, and an extraction of one file reads
only that file.
.PP
The archive is one seekable file. It cannot be read from a pipe or written
to one.
.PP
Changes to an archive add a new generation after the old one. The old
generation stays intact until the new one is complete, so a crash does not
damage what the archive held. A deleted or replaced member stays in the file
as a tombstone until
.B \-\-compact
removes it.
.PP
Paths are stored relative. A leading
.B /
or
.B ..
is removed when a file is added, and the program says so one time. There is
no option to keep an absolute path. Extraction checks each path again, and
it never writes outside its destination, not even through a symbolic link.
.SH OPERATIONS
Give exactly one operation.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Create a new archive. An existing file at the path is replaced only when the
new archive is complete. If the create fails, the old file stays as it was.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-append
Add paths to an archive. A path that is already in the archive is replaced;
.B \-\-on\-conflict
changes that.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Add only the paths whose copy in the archive is out of date.
.B \-\-update\-mode
selects the test.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-list
List the members. With
.BR \-v ,
give the long listing. With
.BR \-vv ,
give more columns and a line of totals.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract
Extract the members, into the current directory or into
.BR \-d .
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Mark the members that match each pattern as deleted. Each pattern must match
a member, or nothing changes.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
Write the archive again without its dead space: tombstones, and old indexes.
.TP
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
Seal the key of an encrypted archive under a new passphrase. The program asks
for the old passphrase, then two times for the new one. It writes the archive
again, as
.B \-\-compact
does. The content is copied, not encrypted again. The Argon2id parameters stay
as they are, except those that a
.B \-\-kdf\-*
option gives.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Decode every member and check every digest and every authentication tag,
without extraction.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
After a crash during a change, cut the file back to the last complete
generation. An archive that opens correctly is not changed.
.TP
.B \-\-info
Show the header, the encryption settings, the generation, the member counts,
the codecs and the dead space.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-codecs
Show each codec with its parameters, defaults and ranges.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Archive and paths
.TP
.BI \-f " archive" "\fR, \fP\-\-file " archive
The archive. It is necessary for every operation except
.B \-\-list\-codecs
and
.BR \-\-show\-config .
.TP
.BI \-d " dir" "\fR, \fP\-\-destination " dir
Extract into
.IR dir ,
and create it if it does not exist.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir" "\fR, \fP\-\-directory " dir
Change to
.I dir
first.
.I dir
must exist.
.TP
.BI \-T " file" "\fR, \fP\-\-files\-from " file
Read the paths from
.IR file ,
or from standard input if
.I file
is
.BR \- .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " glob"
Leave out the paths that match
.IR glob .
An excluded directory is not entered. It can be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-X " file" "\fR, \fP\-\-exclude\-from " file
Read one glob from each line of
.IR file .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-dereference
Store what a symbolic link points to, not the link.
.TP
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
Do not enter other filesystems. A mount point is stored, empty.
.SS Compression
.TP
.BI \-Z " spec" "\fR, \fP\-\-compress " spec
Compress with
.IR spec :
.B none
or
.IR name [: key = value [, key = value ]...].
The default is
.BR zstd .
All the members that one operation adds use the same codec. An archive can
hold members from different codecs.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-gzip
The same as
.BR "\-\-compress gzip" .
.TP
.BR \-J ", " \-\-xz
The same as
.BR "\-\-compress xz" .
.TP
.B \-\-zstd
The same as
.BR "\-\-compress zstd" .
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk\-size " size"
Compress in chunks of
.I size
bytes, from 512 B to 256 MiB. The default is 4 MiB. A size can have a unit:
KiB, MiB or GiB.
.SS Encryption
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-encrypt
Encrypt the new archive. The program asks for the passphrase two times. Only
.B \-c
takes this option: every later operation uses the passphrase of the archive,
until
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
changes it.
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt\-index
Also encrypt the index, which hides the names, sizes and digests.
.TP
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " file"
Read the passphrase from the first line of
.IR file .
If the archive is not encrypted, the program refuses it with exit 3.
.TP
.BI \-\-passphrase\-env " var"
Read the passphrase from the environment variable
.IR var .
Other processes can see the environment, so the program prints a warning.
.TP
.BI \-\-new\-passphrase\-file " file" "\fR, \fP\-\-new\-passphrase\-env " var
With
.BR \-\-change\-passphrase ,
read the new passphrase from the first line of
.IR file ,
or from the environment variable
.IR var .
Without them, the program asks for it.
.TP
.BI \-\-kdf\-time " n" "\fR, \fP\-\-kdf\-memory " kib "\fR, \fP\-\-kdf\-threads " n
The Argon2id cost: passes (1 to 64, default 3), memory in KiB (at most
4194304, default 262144), and threads (default 4). Only
.B \-c
and
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
take these options.
.SS Changes
.TP
.BI \-\-on\-conflict " mode"
With
.BR \-r :
what to do with a path that is already in the archive.
.B replace
(the default),
.BR skip ,
or
.BR error ,
which stops and changes nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-update\-mode " mode"
With
.BR \-u :
.B newer
(the default) takes a file whose modification time is later.
.B different
takes a file whose time or size is different, in either direction.
.B digest
reads each file and takes it if its content is different.
.TP
.BI \-\-recompress " spec"
With
.BR \-\-compact :
encode every member again with
.IR spec .
.TP
.B \-\-quick
With
.BR \-\-verify :
check only the structure, and read no member data.
.SS Extraction
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-existing
Never replace an existing file.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace existing files. This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-newer\-only
Replace an existing file only when the member is newer.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-to\-stdout
Write the content to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve\-permissions
Also restore the setuid, setgid and sticky bits.
.TP
.B \-\-preserve\-owner
Restore the owner. Only root can do this.
.TP
.B \-\-preserve\-devices
Create device nodes. Only root can do this.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-xattrs ", " \-\-no\-acls ", " \-\-no\-owner
When adding, do not record this metadata. When extracting, do not restore
it.
.SS Output and work
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show each member as it is processed. With
.BR \-t ,
see
.BR LISTING .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Show errors only.
.TP
.B \-\-long
With
.BR \-t :
the same as
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR \-t :
a JSON listing with every field, for programs.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show a progress line on standard error, when it is a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-j " n" "\fR, \fP\-\-workers " n
Use
.I n
workers, from 1 to 1024. The default is the number of CPUs.
.TP
.BI \-\-memory\-limit " size"
The memory for data in flight. The default comes from the size of the
machine.
.TP
.BI \-\-spill\-threshold " size"
Above this size, a member waits on disk, not in memory. The default is
32 MiB.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-going
Continue after an error on one member, and exit with 1 at the end.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read the configuration from
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Read no configuration file and no
.B EICTAR_*
variable.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-config
Show each setting, its value and where the value came from, then exit.
.SH LISTING
.B \-tv
gives one line for each member:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\-  alice/staff  240001  1432  99.4%  zstd:level=3  2026\-09\-24 17:59:19  d/text.txt
.RE
.fi
.PP
The columns are the type and mode, the owner, the size, the stored size, the
percentage that compression saved, the codec, the modification time and the
path. A member with no data of its own shows
.B \-
for the stored size, the percentage and the codec. A hardlink shares the
data of its target. The percentage can be less than zero. For example, each
chunk in an encrypted archive carries 16 bytes of authentication tag, which
is more than a tiny file.
.PP
.B \-tvv
adds the chunk count,
.B sealed
for an encrypted member, and the start of the BLAKE3 digest. A last line
gives the totals.
.SH PATTERNS
The operations
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-x ,
.B \-\-verify
and
.BR \-\-delete ,
and
.BR \-\-exclude ,
take patterns. A pattern matches a member when it is the member's path, or
a directory that the member is under, or a glob that matches the whole path
(a
.B *
does not match
.BR / ).
A pattern with no
.B /
also matches any name in the path, at any depth: 
.B *.go
takes
.BR src/main.go ,
and
.B cache
takes
.B a/cache
and everything under it. Each pattern must match at least one member.
.PP
Put paths and patterns that start with
.B \-
after
.BR \-\- .
.SH CODECS
.TP
.B zstd
Zstandard. Parameters:
.B level
1 to 22 (default 3), and
.B long
10 to 30, the window size as a power of 2.
.TP
.BR gzip ", " flate
DEFLATE, in a gzip member or raw. Parameter:
.B level
1 to 9 (default 6).
.TP
.B xz
LZMA2, as in
.BR xz (1),
stored without the .xz container. Parameter:
.B preset
0 to 9 (default 6). It is slower than the C
.BR xz ,
and every preset uses the faster match finder.
.TP
.B s2
S2, of the Snappy family: very fast, with a lower ratio. Parameter:
.B mode
fast, better (default) or best.
.TP
.B none
No compression.
.PP
A chunk that compression makes larger is stored as it is. Examples:
.BR "\-\-compress zstd:level=19,long=27" ,
.BR "\-\-compress xz:preset=9" .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings come from these places. The first has priority:
.IP 1. 4
the command line
.IP 2. 4
.B EICTAR_*
environment variables
.IP 3. 4
the configuration file
.IP 4. 4
the built-in defaults
.PP
The configuration file is the first of:
.BR \-\-config ,
.BR $EICTAR_CONFIG ,
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eictar/config
(or
.BR ~/.config/eictar/config ),
and
.BR ~/.eictarrc .
A file in the current directory is never read. The program refuses a
configuration file that another user owns, or that the group or other users
can write.
.PP
Each line is
.IB key " = " value\fR,\fP
where the key is a long option without its dashes. A
.B #
at the start of a line, or after a space, starts a comment.
.B [codec.\fIname\fP]
sections give default parameters for one codec:
.PP
.nf
.RS
compress   = zstd
workers    = 8
exclude    = *.o
exclude    = .cache

[codec.zstd]
level = 19
.RE
.fi
.PP
A variable is
.B EICTAR_
and the long option in capitals, with
.B _
for
.BR \- ,
for example
.B EICTAR_CHUNK_SIZE=8MiB
and
.BR EICTAR_CODEC_ZSTD_LEVEL=19 .
.PP
A configured value applies only to the operations that use it. The
operation, the archive, the paths and the passphrase never come from a
configuration. An unknown key or variable is an error, so that a spelling
mistake is not ignored.
.B \-v
names the configuration file that was read. Scripts can use
.B \-\-no\-config
so that only the command line decides.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation completed, but some members failed under
.BR \-\-keep\-going .
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: a bad option, a bad configuration, a pattern that matches
nothing, or a conflict under
.BR \-\-on\-conflict=error .
Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 3
The archive failed a check: damaged data, a wrong passphrase, a failed
authentication tag, an unsafe path, or a plain archive where a passphrase
was given.
.TP
.B 4
Any other error, for example an I/O error, or another eictar that is
changing the archive.
.TP
.B 70
An option that this build does not support yet.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EICTAR_CONFIG
The configuration file.
.TP
.BI EICTAR_ option
A setting; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Where the configuration file is.
.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 2
Without
.BR \-\-encrypt\-index ,
the index is readable: names, sizes, times and owners. The digest of each
file is a keyed BLAKE3 hash. Thus a person who has a copy of a file cannot
find out from the digests if the archive holds it.
.IP \(bu 2
An encrypted archive detects changes to its content and to its index. A
plain archive detects damage, but not a person who writes the file again.
To know that an archive is the one that you made, keep its hash in a safe
place.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-O ,
the content goes to standard output while it is decoded. For a damaged
member of a plain archive, some bytes can come out before the digest check
fails. The exit status says so.
.IP \(bu 2
Two eictar processes cannot change one archive at the same time: the second
stops with exit 4. The lock is advisory, and it is not reliable on NFS.
.IP \(bu 2
Every key comes from a random data key, which the passphrase seals.
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
seals the same data key under a new passphrase. The blocks of the old file
can stay on the disk. Thus a person with a copy of the old file, or with the
old passphrase and the disk, can still read what the archive held then.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-update\-mode=newer
misses a file that a restore from backup made older. Use
.B different
for backups.
.SH PLATFORMS
Linux has all the metadata. macOS, FreeBSD, NetBSD and OpenBSD have the
owner, the special bits, the times, hardlinks and symbolic links, with these
limits:
.IP \(bu 2
macOS records no ACLs, and extraction skips named pipes and device nodes
with a notice. APFS refuses a file name that is not UTF-8.
.IP \(bu 2
NetBSD and OpenBSD store a file with holes dense.
.IP \(bu 2
OpenBSD has no extended attributes.
.PP
Extended attribute names are stored as the source platform gives them:
.B user.comment
on Linux,
.B com.apple.quarantine
on macOS. Extraction sets each name that the destination takes, and one
notice lists the others. On other platforms, eictar stores content,
directories and links only.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an encrypted archive of ~/Documents, stored as
.BR Documents/... ,
with strong compression:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-cef home.eictar \-C ~ \-\-compress zstd:level=19 Documents
.RE
.fi
.PP
Add what changed since then, and check the result:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-uf home.eictar \-C ~ \-\-update\-mode different Documents
eictar \-\-verify \-f home.eictar
.RE
.fi
.PP
Extract one directory into another place:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-xf home.eictar \-d /tmp/restore Documents/letters
.RE
.fi
.PP
Remove old versions, and encode everything again with xz:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-\-compact \-f home.eictar \-\-recompress xz:preset=9
.RE
.fi
.PP
Change the passphrase, and make the key derivation stronger:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-\-change\-passphrase \-f home.eictar \-\-kdf\-memory 1048576
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.eictarrc
The configuration file, when there is no other.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1),
.BR zstd (1),
.BR xz (1)
.PP
.I doc/design.md
gives the design, and
.I doc/format.md
specifies the archive format.
